# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Triseno CMS (/edit) — environment
#
# Five values. No database, no third-party service, no accounts to create beyond a
# GitHub token. Set them in Vercel → Project → Settings → Environment Variables
# (Production), then redeploy.
#
# Every one is SERVER-ONLY. None may ever be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, and none may go in
# next.config.ts's `env` key — that inlines values into the client bundle, which would
# publish the GitHub token to every visitor.
#
# Until these are set, /edit simply 404s and login returns 503. The public site is
# completely unaffected either way.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── 1-2. The PIN, scrypt-hashed. Never stored in plaintext, never committed. ──
# To change the PIN:
#   node -e "const c=require('crypto');const s=c.randomBytes(16).toString('hex');console.log('EDIT_PIN_SALT='+s);console.log('EDIT_PIN_HASH='+c.scryptSync('YOUR_PIN',s,64).toString('hex'))"
EDIT_PIN_SALT=
EDIT_PIN_HASH=

# ── 3. Signs the session cookie. ──
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
EDIT_SESSION_SECRET=

# ── 4. The unlock key — the thing that actually secures the editor. ──
# /edit 404s for everyone until they visit, once:
#   https://trisenosystems.com/edit/unlock?k=<this value>
# which drops a year-long cookie. Bookmark that link; treat it like a password.
#
# This is why a 4-digit PIN is safe here: the login endpoint refuses any request without
# the unlock cookie, so an attacker must guess 128 bits before the 10,000 PINs even
# become relevant. Rotate this value to revoke access from every device at once.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(16).toString('hex'))"
EDIT_UNLOCK_KEY=

# ── 5. Publishing. ──
# A GitHub fine-grained personal access token, scoped to ONLY
# Triseno1397/Triseno-Systems-Website, with exactly one permission:
#   Repository permissions → Contents → Read and write
# Nothing else. Publishing commits src/content/reels.json to main; Vercel deploys it.
#
# NOTE: fine-grained tokens EXPIRE. When it does, Publish starts failing with a clear
# "token has probably expired" message and nothing else breaks. Put the expiry date in a
# calendar and generate a new one.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── Local development only ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Drops the __Host- cookie prefix and the Secure flag so the session works over
# http://localhost. MUST NOT be set in production — it downgrades the session cookie.
# CMS_INSECURE_COOKIES=1

# ── Deliberately absent ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is no database and no draft store. Unpublished edits live in the editor's own
# browser (localStorage) and only reach the server when Publish is pressed, which sends
# them straight into a git commit. The trade-off: a draft does not follow him to another
# device. Worth revisiting only if that actually bites.
